<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Chongqing</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Chungking</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Chongqing is a city at the confluence of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yangtze-river">Yangtze</a> and Jialing rivers in south-western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a>, made a municipality directly under the central government in 1997. In ancient times it was a centre of the State of Ba. During the war against <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/japan">Japan</a> from 1937 to 1945 it served as the wartime capital of the Nationalist government and endured years of Japanese bombing.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/chongqing.jpg" alt="Chongqing" width="320" height="240" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: HoweyYuan · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Yuzhong_District_night_view.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Chinese armies lost the Battle of Shanghai, Nanjing fell in December 1937 amid the Nanjing Massacre, and the government withdrew to <b>Chongqing</b>, where it held out until 1945 at a cost of millions of military and civilian lives.</blockquote><a href="/empire/republic-of-china">Republic of China · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>Contemporary Maoists in China criticise the social inequalities created under the party, and some radicals have backed protests and strikes such as the Jasic incident; Bo Xilai’s mass mobilisation with red songs in <b>Chongqing</b> was called neo-Maoist.</blockquote><a href="/ideology/maoism">Maoism · Legacy</a></li><li><blockquote>The Pathé recording appears in Joris Ivens's documentary The 400 Million (1939); Lee Pao-chen printed it with an English translation in a songbook in <b>Chongqing</b> that year, later used in American schools during the war; and the New York Times published its music.</blockquote><a href="/songs/march-of-the-volunteers">March of the Volunteers</a></li><li><blockquote>Ming Yuzhen, a commander of Xu Shouhui's Red Turban regime, led an army up the Yangtze into Sichuan in 1357 and took <b>Chongqing</b>.</blockquote><a href="/state/ming-xia">Ming Xia</a></li><li><blockquote>After full-scale war began in 1937 the government moved to <b>Chongqing</b>, which survived years of bombing; China became one of the Allied powers.</blockquote><a href="/state/nationalist-government">Nationalist government</a></li><li><blockquote>Under Kim Gu it organised attacks on Japanese leaders, including Yun Bong-gil's bombing in Shanghai in 1932, then moved through several Chinese cities before settling in <b>Chongqing</b> in 1940, where it formed the Korean Liberation Army and declared war on Japan in 1941.</blockquote><a href="/state/provisional-government-of-the-republic-of-korea">Provisional Government of the Republic of Korea</a></li></ul></section>
